 operations and copper purification processes at the mines. Sources of Metals Bronze and Tin: Egypt imported ready-made Asian bronze and imported tin from northern countries to mix with copper. Gold: Egyptians mastered the extraction of gold from Nubian mines and fiercely protected that region, treating it as an integral part of their territory. Silver: They also obtained silver, which was more valuable than gold at the time. The magnificent silver coffin of King Psusennes I (Twenty-First Dynasty, c. 990 BC), displayed at the Egyptian Museum, attests to their skill. Lost-Wax Bronze Casting Technique The ancient Egyptians used the brilliant 'lost-wax' method to cast bronze statues: 1. The artist creates a preliminary model of the statue in heat-resistant clay or sand. 2. This model is covered with a layer of wax, on which the fine details of the statue are sculpted. 3. The wax model is coated with an outer layer of clay, leaving two openings — one for pouring metal and one for air to escape. 4. The mold is heated until the wax melts and flows out, leaving a cavity holding the finest details of the statue. 5. Molten metal (bronze or copper) is poured to replace the wax; once cooled and the clay mold broken, the metal statue emerges in its final form. Contents of the Display Case (A): A clay figurine found in the tomb of King Ramesses XI. (B): A wax figurine also found in the tomb of Ramesses XI (these two represent preparatory stages of the lost-wax casting process). Three completed bronze statues: a statue of the god Amun; a statue of the god Horus as a falcon; and a statue of the goddess Isis nursing her son Horus."
