sed to rinse pens and erase mistakes. A Box or Case: For storing pens and inks. The scribe tied all his writing tools together with string — even in hieroglyphic writing, the symbol for writing tools is drawn tied in this manner. Writing was a skill reserved for the elite, so princes, nobles, and sometimes kings would boast of their ability to write. The word \"scribe\" was pronounced \"sesh\" and written in two known forms. Interestingly, Book of the Dead inscriptions from the New Kingdom and Late Period often show the scribe's palette or toolbox as part of the offerings presented to the deceased — even if the deceased himself could not read or write — showing the deceased receiving writing tools from Thoth, god of writing, for use in the afterlife. Contents of the Ancient_Egyptian_Scribe's_Palette_Painting_Tools (A): A wooden and bronze statuette of the god Thoth in the form of an ibis bird, dating to the Greco-Roman period, found at Tuna el-Gebel. Note: The ibis was one of the forms in which Thoth, god of writing and knowledge, was represented. Thoth was closely associated with the court of Osiris in the afterlife — in the traditional scene of the Weighing of the Heart, Thoth oversees the process. The ibis belongs to a family of long-legged birds that live in marshes and muddy land. The Egyptian farmer called it 'Abu Minjal' (father of the sickle) because its beak resembles a sickle, helping it catch worms and insects. The farmer marveled at the bird's ability to detect worms hidden in the mud, viewing this as a divine gift — which is why it was chosen to represent the god of knowledge. (B): A wooden board on which the scribe placed his pigments. (C): An ivory pen case with a slot for pens and two hollows containing residues of black and red pigments. (D): A block of black manganese oxide and red manganese oxide, used to produce inks. (F): A pen case for storing pens and inks."
