 them using oars to steer the ship. Their navigational mastery enabled them to sail beyond the Nile (stretching approximately 1,200 km) and into the Red Sea and Mediterranean (along 2,000 km of coastline). Temple and tomb walls documented various types of vessels, including: transport and fishing boats; troop transport ships (used in foreign wars, making Egyptian civilization the first in history to use troop transport ships); and religious and funerary boats (dedicated to temples or for transporting mummies to the sacred site of Abydos). Religious Significance of Boat Models in Tombs The ancient Egyptian believed the afterlife was a reflection of earthly life, and that there would be a river resembling the Nile. Since placing real boats inside tombs was impractical, they placed miniature wooden or clay models. The prevailing belief was that the dimensions of the afterlife differed from those of this world, and that these small models would be magically transformed to their full size to help the deceased navigate the rivers of the afterlife. This tradition continued to the end of Egyptian civilization. The two boat models displayed here date to the Middle Kingdom (Eleventh Dynasty) and were discovered in the tombs of the Meir district in Asyut Governorate. Each boat contains a set of oars, indicating the boat is in a southward sailing position (against the current) — oars were used to sail south against the current, while sails were used to sail north with the current. The crew consists of rowers, a captain always standing at the bow, and an overseer positioned at the stern."
