gs. His blessed memory lived on among Egyptians as one of the greatest sages, to the degree that he was elevated to the status of a god in the Ptolemaic period (300-30 BC). Long Life and Other Statues This exceptional man lived for more than 80 years. Two other statues of him are displayed at the Egyptian Museum in Cairo — one depicting him as a young man, another as an old man. Remarkably, one of the inscriptions on his young-man statue recorded his wish to live to the age of eighty, and after reaching that age, he recorded on another statue that he had indeed attained the age he had hoped for. Architectural Achievements (the Colossi of Memnon) The inscription on this statue indicates that Amenhotep Son of Hapu, in his capacity as Director of Royal Works, prepared a number of colossal statues for his king Amenhotep III. Archaeologists believe this passage refers specifically to his supervision of the carving and transportation of the famous \"Colossi of Memnon,\" erected in front of the king's funerary temple in the Qurna area on the West Bank of Luxor."
